{"parentUuid":"06c2c9dc-298c-4c3e-b576-4f31163c4dfd","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"e89011e8-eb1e-4bfc-8125-a45c3d943a16","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"validated-dancing-bee","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_014hzc5UYuovXoGUzZAoftXM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01Xhr6KWEMDQLxJfjhSTfKLc","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/root/environment/skills/rdkit-morgan-fingerprints.md","content":"# RDKit Morgan Fingerprints & Tanimoto Similarity\n\n## Overview\nMorgan fingerprints (also known as circular fingerprints or ECFP) are molecular fingerprints computed using the Morgan algorithm in RDKit. They encode local molecular environments around each atom. Tanimoto similarity (also called Jaccard similarity) is the standard metric for comparing molecular fingerprints.\n\n## Installation\nRDKit can be installed via conda or pip:\n```bash\nconda install -c conda-forge rdkit\n# or\npip install rdkit-pypi\n```\n\n## Key Imports\n```python\nfrom rdkit import Chem\nfrom rdkit.Chem import AllChem\nfrom rdkit import DataStructs\n```\n\n## Creating Morgan Fingerprints\n```python\n# From a SMILES string\nmol = Chem.MolFromSmiles('CC(=O)OC1=CC=CC=C1C(=O)O')  # Aspirin\n\n# Generate Morgan fingerprint\n# radius=2 is equivalent to ECFP4\n# useChirality=True includes stereochemistry information\nfp = AllChem.GetMorganFingerprintAsBitVect(\n    mol,\n    radius=2,\n    nBits=2048,        # default bit vector length\n    useChirality=True  # include chirality\n)\n```\n\n## Parameters\n- **radius**: The radius of the Morgan algorithm. radius=2 gives ECFP4 (diameter 4).\n- **nBits**: Length of the bit vector (default 2048).\n- **useChirality**: If True, includes chirality information in the fingerprint.\n- **useFeatures**: If True, uses pharmacophoric features instead of atom types (gives FCFP instead of ECFP).\n\n## Computing Tanimoto Similarity\n```python\n# Tanimoto similarity between two fingerprints\nsimilarity = DataStructs.TanimotoSimilarity(fp1, fp2)\n# Returns a float between 0.0 (no similarity) and 1.0 (identical)\n```\n\n## Bulk Tanimoto Similarity\n```python\n# Compare one fingerprint against many\nsimilarities = DataStructs.BulkTanimotoSimilarity(target_fp, list_of_fps)\n# Returns a list of floats\n```\n\n## Complete Example: Finding Top-K Similar Molecules\n```python\nfrom rdkit import Chem\nfrom rdkit.Chem import AllChem\nfrom rdkit import DataStructs\n\ndef compute_similarity(smiles1, smiles2, radius=2, use_chirality=True):\n    \"\"\"Compute Tanimoto similarity between two molecules given as SMILES.\"\"\"\n    mol1 = Chem.MolFromSmiles(smiles1)\n    mol2 = Chem.MolFromSmiles(smiles2)\n\n    if mol1 is None or mol2 is None:\n        return 0.0\n\n    fp1 = AllChem.GetMorganFingerprintAsBitVect(mol1, radius, useChirality=use_chirality)\n    fp2 = AllChem.GetMorganFingerprintAsBitVect(mol2, radius, useChirality=use_chirality)\n\n    return DataStructs.TanimotoSimilarity(fp1, fp2)\n\n# Example usage\naspirin = 'CC(=O)OC1=CC=CC=C1C(=O)O'\nibuprofen = 'CC(C)CC1=CC=C(C=C1)C(C)C(=O)O'\nsimilarity = compute_similarity(aspirin, ibuprofen)\nprint(f\"Similarity: {similarity:.4f}\")\n```\n\n## Notes\n- Morgan fingerprints with radius=2 are equivalent to ECFP4 fingerprints.\n- The default nBits=2048 is standard; higher values reduce collision but increase computation.\n- Always validate molecules with `Chem.MolFromSmiles()` before computing fingerprints.\n- Tanimoto similarity ranges from 0 (completely different) to 1 (identical fingerprints).\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":220,"cache_read_input_tokens":36110,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":220,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXt8mVUcDqpg4KtrT327t","type":"assistant","uuid":"333c61d2-d099-48d1-bc1e-8a0c7ce34426","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:12:10.987Z"}
{"parentUuid":"333c61d2-d099-48d1-bc1e-8a0c7ce34426","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"e89011e8-eb1e-4bfc-8125-a45c3d943a16","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"validated-dancing-bee","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_014hzc5UYuovXoGUzZAoftXM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01Pm2SKt4aq58fkSeSFNVN5g","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/root/environment/skills/pubchempy-name-to-smiles.md","content":"# PubChemPy: Chemical Name to SMILES Conversion\n\n## Overview\nPubChemPy is a Python wrapper for the PubChem PUG REST API. It allows querying PubChem's database to convert chemical names into molecular representations (SMILES, InChI, molecular formula, etc.) without manually maintaining a name-to-SMILES mapping.\n\n## Installation\n```bash\npip install pubchempy\n```\n\n## Key Imports\n```python\nimport pubchempy as pcp\n```\n\n## Basic Usage: Name to SMILES\n\n```python\nimport pubchempy as pcp\n\ndef get_smiles_from_name(chemical_name):\n    \"\"\"Convert a chemical name to its canonical SMILES representation.\"\"\"\n    compounds = pcp.get_compounds(chemical_name, 'name')\n    if compounds:\n        return compounds[0].canonical_smiles\n    return None\n\n# Example\nsmiles = get_smiles_from_name('aspirin')\nprint(smiles)  # CC(=O)OC1=CC=CC=C1C(=O)O\n```\n\n## Key Properties of Compound Objects\n```python\ncompound = pcp.get_compounds('caffeine', 'name')[0]\n\ncompound.canonical_smiles   # Canonical SMILES\ncompound.isomeric_smiles    # SMILES with stereochemistry (deprecated, use .smiles)\ncompound.smiles             # Preferred SMILES property\ncompound.iupac_name         # IUPAC name\ncompound.molecular_formula  # Molecular formula\ncompound.molecular_weight   # Molecular weight\ncompound.inchi              # InChI string\ncompound.inchikey            # InChI key\ncompound.cid                # PubChem Compound ID\n```\n\n## Searching by Different Identifiers\n```python\n# By name\npcp.get_compounds('aspirin', 'name')\n\n# By SMILES\npcp.get_compounds('CC(=O)OC1=CC=CC=C1C(=O)O', 'smiles')\n\n# By CID\npcp.Compound.from_cid(2244)  # Aspirin's CID\n\n# By InChI\npcp.get_compounds('InChI=1S/C9H8O4/...', 'inchi')\n```\n\n## Handling Errors and Edge Cases\n```python\nimport pubchempy as pcp\n\ndef safe_get_smiles(name):\n    \"\"\"Safely get SMILES with error handling.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        compounds = pcp.get_compounds(name, 'name')\n        if compounds:\n            return compounds[0].canonical_smiles\n        else:\n            print(f\"No compound found for: {name}\")\n            return None\n    except pcp.PubChemHTTPError as e:\n        print(f\"PubChem API error for {name}: {e}\")\n        return None\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"Error looking up {name}: {e}\")\n        return None\n```\n\n## Batch Processing Chemical Names\n```python\ndef batch_get_smiles(names):\n    \"\"\"Convert a list of chemical names to SMILES.\"\"\"\n    results = {}\n    for name in names:\n        smiles = safe_get_smiles(name)\n        if smiles:\n            results[name] = smiles\n    return results\n```\n\n## Rate Limiting\nPubChem has rate limits (~5 requests/second). For large batches:\n```python\nimport time\n\ndef batch_get_smiles_with_delay(names, delay=0.2):\n    \"\"\"Convert names to SMILES with rate limiting.\"\"\"\n    results = {}\n    for name in names:\n        smiles = safe_get_smiles(name)\n        if smiles:\n            results[name] = smiles\n        time.sleep(delay)  # Respect rate limits\n    return results\n```\n\n## Notes\n- PubChem is a free, public database maintained by NCBI/NIH.\n- The API does not require authentication.\n- Results are sorted by relevance; the first result is usually the best match.\n- `canonical_smiles` is preferred over `isomeric_smiles` for consistency.\n- Network access is required; consider caching results for repeated lookups.\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":220,"cache_read_input_tokens":36110,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":220,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXt8mVUcDqpg4KtrT327t","type":"assistant","uuid":"40e34add-3353-4b38-bd04-cad53ec2d884","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:12:24.843Z"}
{"parentUuid":"40e34add-3353-4b38-bd04-cad53ec2d884","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"e89011e8-eb1e-4bfc-8125-a45c3d943a16","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"validated-dancing-bee","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_014hzc5UYuovXoGUzZAoftXM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_0123fCQJXJUMUfbJ5CdWYDdv","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/root/environment/skills/pdf-text-extraction-pymupdf.md","content":"# PDF Text Extraction with PyMuPDF (fitz)\n\n## Overview\nPyMuPDF (imported as `fitz`) is a fast, lightweight Python library for reading and manipulating PDF files. It provides reliable text extraction from PDF documents.\n\n## Installation\n```bash\npip install pymupdf\n```\n\n## Key Imports\n```python\nimport fitz  # PyMuPDF\n```\n\n## Basic Text Extraction\n```python\nimport fitz\n\ndef extract_text_from_pdf(filepath):\n    \"\"\"Extract all text from a PDF file.\"\"\"\n    doc = fitz.open(filepath)\n    full_text = \"\"\n    for page in doc:\n        full_text += page.get_text()\n    doc.close()\n    return full_text\n```\n\n## Extracting Lines/Items from PDF\n```python\ndef extract_lines_from_pdf(filepath):\n    \"\"\"Extract non-empty lines from a PDF file.\"\"\"\n    doc = fitz.open(filepath)\n    lines = []\n    for page in doc:\n        text = page.get_text()\n        for line in text.strip().split('\\n'):\n            line = line.strip()\n            if line:\n                lines.append(line)\n    doc.close()\n    return lines\n```\n\n## Page-by-Page Extraction\n```python\ndef extract_pages(filepath):\n    \"\"\"Extract text from each page separately.\"\"\"\n    doc = fitz.open(filepath)\n    pages = []\n    for page_num in range(len(doc)):\n        page = doc[page_num]\n        pages.append(page.get_text())\n    doc.close()\n    return pages\n```\n\n## Notes\n- PyMuPDF is one of the fastest PDF libraries for Python.\n- `get_text()` returns plain text; use `get_text(\"html\")` for HTML or `get_text(\"dict\")` for structured data.\n- Always close documents with `doc.close()` or use context managers.\n- Works with most standard PDFs; may struggle with scanned documents (use OCR for those).\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":220,"cache_read_input_tokens":36110,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":220,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXt8mVUcDqpg4KtrT327t","type":"assistant","uuid":"37190ad1-05ea-44f8-ac7e-41fb8a5fcc23","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:12:31.317Z"}
{"parentUuid":"37190ad1-05ea-44f8-ac7e-41fb8a5fcc23","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"e89011e8-eb1e-4bfc-8125-a45c3d943a16","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"validated-dancing-bee","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_014hzc5UYuovXoGUzZAoftXM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01QhRPP4DauaeZ68WPik1DCP","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/root/environment/skills/tanimoto-similarity-ranking.md","content":"# Tanimoto Similarity Ranking for Chemical Search\n\n## Overview\nThis skill covers the pattern of ranking a pool of molecules by their Tanimoto similarity to a target molecule, and returning the top-K most similar ones. This is a common cheminformatics task used in virtual screening, lead optimization, and molecular library analysis.\n\n## Algorithm\n1. Convert the target molecule name to a SMILES string (via PubChem or other database).\n2. Parse the pool of molecule names from a data source (e.g., PDF, CSV).\n3. Convert each pool molecule name to SMILES.\n4. Compute Morgan fingerprints for all molecules.\n5. Calculate Tanimoto similarity between the target and each pool molecule.\n6. Sort by descending similarity, with alphabetical tie-breaking.\n7. Return the top-K results.\n\n## Sorting with Tie-Breaking\nWhen two molecules have the same Tanimoto similarity, sort alphabetically by name:\n```python\n# Sort by (-similarity, name) for descending similarity with alphabetical tie-breaking\nresults.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[1], x[0]))\n```\n\n## Complete Implementation Pattern\n```python\nfrom rdkit import Chem\nfrom rdkit.Chem import AllChem\nfrom rdkit import DataStructs\nimport pubchempy as pcp\n\ndef name_to_smiles(name):\n    \"\"\"Convert chemical name to SMILES via PubChem.\"\"\"\n    compounds = pcp.get_compounds(name, 'name')\n    if compounds:\n        return compounds[0].canonical_smiles\n    return None\n\ndef compute_morgan_fp(smiles, radius=2, use_chirality=True):\n    \"\"\"Compute Morgan fingerprint from SMILES.\"\"\"\n    mol = Chem.MolFromSmiles(smiles)\n    if mol is None:\n        return None\n    return AllChem.GetMorganFingerprintAsBitVect(\n        mol, radius=radius, useChirality=use_chirality\n    )\n\ndef topk_similar(target_name, pool_names, k):\n    \"\"\"Find top-k most similar molecules to target.\"\"\"\n    target_smiles = name_to_smiles(target_name)\n    target_fp = compute_morgan_fp(target_smiles)\n\n    results = []\n    for name in pool_names:\n        smiles = name_to_smiles(name)\n        if smiles is None:\n            continue\n        fp = compute_morgan_fp(smiles)\n        if fp is None:\n            continue\n        sim = DataStructs.TanimotoSimilarity(target_fp, fp)\n        results.append((name, sim))\n\n    # Sort: descending similarity, then alphabetical name\n    results.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[1], x[0]))\n    return results[:k]\n```\n\n## Performance Considerations\n- Cache SMILES lookups to avoid redundant PubChem API calls.\n- Use `DataStructs.BulkTanimotoSimilarity()` for computing similarity against many molecules at once.\n- Pre-compute fingerprints for the pool if doing multiple queries.\n\n## Output Format\nTypical output is a list of tuples or dicts:\n```python\n[\n    (\"Aspirin\", 1.0),\n    (\"Methyl salicylate\", 0.45),\n    (\"Salicylic acid\", 0.42),\n]\n```\n\n## Edge Cases\n- Target molecule not found in PubChem: raise an error or return empty.\n- Pool molecule not found: skip it.\n- k larger than pool: return all valid results.\n- Duplicate names in pool: handle according to requirements.\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":220,"cache_read_input_tokens":36110,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":220,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXt8mVUcDqpg4KtrT327t","type":"assistant","uuid":"10d84345-0fa4-495e-b38b-2b45aca5c867","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:12:45.290Z"}
{"parentUuid":"10d84345-0fa4-495e-b38b-2b45aca5c867","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"e89011e8-eb1e-4bfc-8125-a45c3d943a16","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"validated-dancing-bee","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_014hzc5UYuovXoGUzZAoftXM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01WaNHqEcb1q7eEsAAzCUAiQ","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/root/environment/skills/cirpy-chemical-name-resolver.md","content":"# CIRpy: Chemical Name Resolution (Fallback)\n\n## Overview\nCIRpy is a Python interface to the NIH Chemical Identifier Resolver (CIR). It can convert chemical names to SMILES, InChI, and other representations. Useful as a fallback when PubChemPy doesn't find a compound.\n\n## Installation\n```bash\npip install cirpy\n```\n\n## Basic Usage\n```python\nimport cirpy\n\n# Name to SMILES\nsmiles = cirpy.resolve('aspirin', 'smiles')\nprint(smiles)  # OC(=O)c1ccccc1OC(C)=O\n\n# Name to InChI\ninchi = cirpy.resolve('aspirin', 'inchi')\n```\n\n## Supported Representations\n- `'smiles'` - SMILES string\n- `'inchi'` - InChI string\n- `'iupac_name'` - IUPAC name\n- `'formula'` - Molecular formula\n- `'mw'` - Molecular weight\n\n## Fallback Strategy\n```python\nimport pubchempy as pcp\nimport cirpy\n\ndef robust_name_to_smiles(name):\n    \"\"\"Try PubChem first, fall back to CIR.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        compounds = pcp.get_compounds(name, 'name')\n        if compounds:\n            return compounds[0].canonical_smiles\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n\n    try:\n        smiles = cirpy.resolve(name, 'smiles')\n        if smiles:\n            return smiles\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n\n    return None\n```\n\n## Notes\n- CIR is maintained by NIH/NCI.\n- No authentication required.\n- Can be slower than PubChem for batch queries.\n- Good fallback for names PubChem doesn't recognize.\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":220,"cache_read_input_tokens":36110,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":220,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXt8mVUcDqpg4KtrT327t","type":"assistant","uuid":"0510d43c-6a75-4605-8e29-4f8214c64e0c","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:12:52.050Z"}
{"parentUuid":"333c61d2-d099-48d1-bc1e-8a0c7ce34426","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"e89011e8-eb1e-4bfc-8125-a45c3d943a16","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"validated-dancing-bee","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01Xhr6KWEMDQLxJfjhSTfKLc","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /root/environment/skills/rdkit-morgan-fingerprints.md"}]},"uuid":"c75418a9-26af-4e1e-ad0a-4943391fd6a6","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:12:52.104Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/root/environment/skills/rdkit-morgan-fingerprints.md","content":"# RDKit Morgan Fingerprints & Tanimoto Similarity\n\n## Overview\nMorgan fingerprints (also known as circular fingerprints or ECFP) are molecular fingerprints computed using the Morgan algorithm in RDKit. They encode local molecular environments around each atom. Tanimoto similarity (also called Jaccard similarity) is the standard metric for comparing molecular fingerprints.\n\n## Installation\nRDKit can be installed via conda or pip:\n```bash\nconda install -c conda-forge rdkit\n# or\npip install rdkit-pypi\n```\n\n## Key Imports\n```python\nfrom rdkit import Chem\nfrom rdkit.Chem import AllChem\nfrom rdkit import DataStructs\n```\n\n## Creating Morgan Fingerprints\n```python\n# From a SMILES string\nmol = Chem.MolFromSmiles('CC(=O)OC1=CC=CC=C1C(=O)O')  # Aspirin\n\n# Generate Morgan fingerprint\n# radius=2 is equivalent to ECFP4\n# useChirality=True includes stereochemistry information\nfp = AllChem.GetMorganFingerprintAsBitVect(\n    mol,\n    radius=2,\n    nBits=2048,        # default bit vector length\n    useChirality=True  # include chirality\n)\n```\n\n## Parameters\n- **radius**: The radius of the Morgan algorithm. radius=2 gives ECFP4 (diameter 4).\n- **nBits**: Length of the bit vector (default 2048).\n- **useChirality**: If True, includes chirality information in the fingerprint.\n- **useFeatures**: If True, uses pharmacophoric features instead of atom types (gives FCFP instead of ECFP).\n\n## Computing Tanimoto Similarity\n```python\n# Tanimoto similarity between two fingerprints\nsimilarity = DataStructs.TanimotoSimilarity(fp1, fp2)\n# Returns a float between 0.0 (no similarity) and 1.0 (identical)\n```\n\n## Bulk Tanimoto Similarity\n```python\n# Compare one fingerprint against many\nsimilarities = DataStructs.BulkTanimotoSimilarity(target_fp, list_of_fps)\n# Returns a list of floats\n```\n\n## Complete Example: Finding Top-K Similar Molecules\n```python\nfrom rdkit import Chem\nfrom rdkit.Chem import AllChem\nfrom rdkit import DataStructs\n\ndef compute_similarity(smiles1, smiles2, radius=2, use_chirality=True):\n    \"\"\"Compute Tanimoto similarity between two molecules given as SMILES.\"\"\"\n    mol1 = Chem.MolFromSmiles(smiles1)\n    mol2 = Chem.MolFromSmiles(smiles2)\n\n    if mol1 is None or mol2 is None:\n        return 0.0\n\n    fp1 = AllChem.GetMorganFingerprintAsBitVect(mol1, radius, useChirality=use_chirality)\n    fp2 = AllChem.GetMorganFingerprintAsBitVect(mol2, radius, useChirality=use_chirality)\n\n    return DataStructs.TanimotoSimilarity(fp1, fp2)\n\n# Example usage\naspirin = 'CC(=O)OC1=CC=CC=C1C(=O)O'\nibuprofen = 'CC(C)CC1=CC=C(C=C1)C(C)C(=O)O'\nsimilarity = compute_similarity(aspirin, ibuprofen)\nprint(f\"Similarity: {similarity:.4f}\")\n```\n\n## Notes\n- Morgan fingerprints with radius=2 are equivalent to ECFP4 fingerprints.\n- The default nBits=2048 is standard; higher values reduce collision but increase computation.\n- Always validate molecules with `Chem.MolFromSmiles()` before computing fingerprints.\n- Tanimoto similarity ranges from 0 (completely different) to 1 (identical fingerprints).\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"333c61d2-d099-48d1-bc1e-8a0c7ce34426"}
{"parentUuid":"40e34add-3353-4b38-bd04-cad53ec2d884","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"e89011e8-eb1e-4bfc-8125-a45c3d943a16","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"validated-dancing-bee","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01Pm2SKt4aq58fkSeSFNVN5g","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /root/environment/skills/pubchempy-name-to-smiles.md"}]},"uuid":"5063191e-0b17-4fcc-b28a-0312b60b32b7","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:12:52.117Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/root/environment/skills/pubchempy-name-to-smiles.md","content":"# PubChemPy: Chemical Name to SMILES Conversion\n\n## Overview\nPubChemPy is a Python wrapper for the PubChem PUG REST API. It allows querying PubChem's database to convert chemical names into molecular representations (SMILES, InChI, molecular formula, etc.) without manually maintaining a name-to-SMILES mapping.\n\n## Installation\n```bash\npip install pubchempy\n```\n\n## Key Imports\n```python\nimport pubchempy as pcp\n```\n\n## Basic Usage: Name to SMILES\n\n```python\nimport pubchempy as pcp\n\ndef get_smiles_from_name(chemical_name):\n    \"\"\"Convert a chemical name to its canonical SMILES representation.\"\"\"\n    compounds = pcp.get_compounds(chemical_name, 'name')\n    if compounds:\n        return compounds[0].canonical_smiles\n    return None\n\n# Example\nsmiles = get_smiles_from_name('aspirin')\nprint(smiles)  # CC(=O)OC1=CC=CC=C1C(=O)O\n```\n\n## Key Properties of Compound Objects\n```python\ncompound = pcp.get_compounds('caffeine', 'name')[0]\n\ncompound.canonical_smiles   # Canonical SMILES\ncompound.isomeric_smiles    # SMILES with stereochemistry (deprecated, use .smiles)\ncompound.smiles             # Preferred SMILES property\ncompound.iupac_name         # IUPAC name\ncompound.molecular_formula  # Molecular formula\ncompound.molecular_weight   # Molecular weight\ncompound.inchi              # InChI string\ncompound.inchikey            # InChI key\ncompound.cid                # PubChem Compound ID\n```\n\n## Searching by Different Identifiers\n```python\n# By name\npcp.get_compounds('aspirin', 'name')\n\n# By SMILES\npcp.get_compounds('CC(=O)OC1=CC=CC=C1C(=O)O', 'smiles')\n\n# By CID\npcp.Compound.from_cid(2244)  # Aspirin's CID\n\n# By InChI\npcp.get_compounds('InChI=1S/C9H8O4/...', 'inchi')\n```\n\n## Handling Errors and Edge Cases\n```python\nimport pubchempy as pcp\n\ndef safe_get_smiles(name):\n    \"\"\"Safely get SMILES with error handling.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        compounds = pcp.get_compounds(name, 'name')\n        if compounds:\n            return compounds[0].canonical_smiles\n        else:\n            print(f\"No compound found for: {name}\")\n            return None\n    except pcp.PubChemHTTPError as e:\n        print(f\"PubChem API error for {name}: {e}\")\n        return None\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"Error looking up {name}: {e}\")\n        return None\n```\n\n## Batch Processing Chemical Names\n```python\ndef batch_get_smiles(names):\n    \"\"\"Convert a list of chemical names to SMILES.\"\"\"\n    results = {}\n    for name in names:\n        smiles = safe_get_smiles(name)\n        if smiles:\n            results[name] = smiles\n    return results\n```\n\n## Rate Limiting\nPubChem has rate limits (~5 requests/second). For large batches:\n```python\nimport time\n\ndef batch_get_smiles_with_delay(names, delay=0.2):\n    \"\"\"Convert names to SMILES with rate limiting.\"\"\"\n    results = {}\n    for name in names:\n        smiles = safe_get_smiles(name)\n        if smiles:\n            results[name] = smiles\n        time.sleep(delay)  # Respect rate limits\n    return results\n```\n\n## Notes\n- PubChem is a free, public database maintained by NCBI/NIH.\n- The API does not require authentication.\n- Results are sorted by relevance; the first result is usually the best match.\n- `canonical_smiles` is preferred over `isomeric_smiles` for consistency.\n- Network access is required; consider caching results for repeated lookups.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"40e34add-3353-4b38-bd04-cad53ec2d884"}
{"parentUuid":"37190ad1-05ea-44f8-ac7e-41fb8a5fcc23","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"e89011e8-eb1e-4bfc-8125-a45c3d943a16","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"validated-dancing-bee","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_0123fCQJXJUMUfbJ5CdWYDdv","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /root/environment/skills/pdf-text-extraction-pymupdf.md"}]},"uuid":"3a568700-5699-4792-9e7a-a10d131a6e7f","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:12:52.132Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/root/environment/skills/pdf-text-extraction-pymupdf.md","content":"# PDF Text Extraction with PyMuPDF (fitz)\n\n## Overview\nPyMuPDF (imported as `fitz`) is a fast, lightweight Python library for reading and manipulating PDF files. It provides reliable text extraction from PDF documents.\n\n## Installation\n```bash\npip install pymupdf\n```\n\n## Key Imports\n```python\nimport fitz  # PyMuPDF\n```\n\n## Basic Text Extraction\n```python\nimport fitz\n\ndef extract_text_from_pdf(filepath):\n    \"\"\"Extract all text from a PDF file.\"\"\"\n    doc = fitz.open(filepath)\n    full_text = \"\"\n    for page in doc:\n        full_text += page.get_text()\n    doc.close()\n    return full_text\n```\n\n## Extracting Lines/Items from PDF\n```python\ndef extract_lines_from_pdf(filepath):\n    \"\"\"Extract non-empty lines from a PDF file.\"\"\"\n    doc = fitz.open(filepath)\n    lines = []\n    for page in doc:\n        text = page.get_text()\n        for line in text.strip().split('\\n'):\n            line = line.strip()\n            if line:\n                lines.append(line)\n    doc.close()\n    return lines\n```\n\n## Page-by-Page Extraction\n```python\ndef extract_pages(filepath):\n    \"\"\"Extract text from each page separately.\"\"\"\n    doc = fitz.open(filepath)\n    pages = []\n    for page_num in range(len(doc)):\n        page = doc[page_num]\n        pages.append(page.get_text())\n    doc.close()\n    return pages\n```\n\n## Notes\n- PyMuPDF is one of the fastest PDF libraries for Python.\n- `get_text()` returns plain text; use `get_text(\"html\")` for HTML or `get_text(\"dict\")` for structured data.\n- Always close documents with `doc.close()` or use context managers.\n- Works with most standard PDFs; may struggle with scanned documents (use OCR for those).\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"37190ad1-05ea-44f8-ac7e-41fb8a5fcc23"}
{"parentUuid":"10d84345-0fa4-495e-b38b-2b45aca5c867","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"e89011e8-eb1e-4bfc-8125-a45c3d943a16","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"validated-dancing-bee","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01QhRPP4DauaeZ68WPik1DCP","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /root/environment/skills/tanimoto-similarity-ranking.md"}]},"uuid":"e6fef81e-51bc-4f6d-9485-2a1c3052be34","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:12:52.147Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/root/environment/skills/tanimoto-similarity-ranking.md","content":"# Tanimoto Similarity Ranking for Chemical Search\n\n## Overview\nThis skill covers the pattern of ranking a pool of molecules by their Tanimoto similarity to a target molecule, and returning the top-K most similar ones. This is a common cheminformatics task used in virtual screening, lead optimization, and molecular library analysis.\n\n## Algorithm\n1. Convert the target molecule name to a SMILES string (via PubChem or other database).\n2. Parse the pool of molecule names from a data source (e.g., PDF, CSV).\n3. Convert each pool molecule name to SMILES.\n4. Compute Morgan fingerprints for all molecules.\n5. Calculate Tanimoto similarity between the target and each pool molecule.\n6. Sort by descending similarity, with alphabetical tie-breaking.\n7. Return the top-K results.\n\n## Sorting with Tie-Breaking\nWhen two molecules have the same Tanimoto similarity, sort alphabetically by name:\n```python\n# Sort by (-similarity, name) for descending similarity with alphabetical tie-breaking\nresults.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[1], x[0]))\n```\n\n## Complete Implementation Pattern\n```python\nfrom rdkit import Chem\nfrom rdkit.Chem import AllChem\nfrom rdkit import DataStructs\nimport pubchempy as pcp\n\ndef name_to_smiles(name):\n    \"\"\"Convert chemical name to SMILES via PubChem.\"\"\"\n    compounds = pcp.get_compounds(name, 'name')\n    if compounds:\n        return compounds[0].canonical_smiles\n    return None\n\ndef compute_morgan_fp(smiles, radius=2, use_chirality=True):\n    \"\"\"Compute Morgan fingerprint from SMILES.\"\"\"\n    mol = Chem.MolFromSmiles(smiles)\n    if mol is None:\n        return None\n    return AllChem.GetMorganFingerprintAsBitVect(\n        mol, radius=radius, useChirality=use_chirality\n    )\n\ndef topk_similar(target_name, pool_names, k):\n    \"\"\"Find top-k most similar molecules to target.\"\"\"\n    target_smiles = name_to_smiles(target_name)\n    target_fp = compute_morgan_fp(target_smiles)\n\n    results = []\n    for name in pool_names:\n        smiles = name_to_smiles(name)\n        if smiles is None:\n            continue\n        fp = compute_morgan_fp(smiles)\n        if fp is None:\n            continue\n        sim = DataStructs.TanimotoSimilarity(target_fp, fp)\n        results.append((name, sim))\n\n    # Sort: descending similarity, then alphabetical name\n    results.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[1], x[0]))\n    return results[:k]\n```\n\n## Performance Considerations\n- Cache SMILES lookups to avoid redundant PubChem API calls.\n- Use `DataStructs.BulkTanimotoSimilarity()` for computing similarity against many molecules at once.\n- Pre-compute fingerprints for the pool if doing multiple queries.\n\n## Output Format\nTypical output is a list of tuples or dicts:\n```python\n[\n    (\"Aspirin\", 1.0),\n    (\"Methyl salicylate\", 0.45),\n    (\"Salicylic acid\", 0.42),\n]\n```\n\n## Edge Cases\n- Target molecule not found in PubChem: raise an error or return empty.\n- Pool molecule not found: skip it.\n- k larger than pool: return all valid results.\n- Duplicate names in pool: handle according to requirements.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"10d84345-0fa4-495e-b38b-2b45aca5c867"}
{"parentUuid":"0510d43c-6a75-4605-8e29-4f8214c64e0c","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"e89011e8-eb1e-4bfc-8125-a45c3d943a16","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"validated-dancing-bee","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01WaNHqEcb1q7eEsAAzCUAiQ","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /root/environment/skills/cirpy-chemical-name-resolver.md"}]},"uuid":"3512962c-9baa-49ce-a381-4671da585523","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:12:52.160Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/root/environment/skills/cirpy-chemical-name-resolver.md","content":"# CIRpy: Chemical Name Resolution (Fallback)\n\n## Overview\nCIRpy is a Python interface to the NIH Chemical Identifier Resolver (CIR). It can convert chemical names to SMILES, InChI, and other representations. Useful as a fallback when PubChemPy doesn't find a compound.\n\n## Installation\n```bash\npip install cirpy\n```\n\n## Basic Usage\n```python\nimport cirpy\n\n# Name to SMILES\nsmiles = cirpy.resolve('aspirin', 'smiles')\nprint(smiles)  # OC(=O)c1ccccc1OC(C)=O\n\n# Name to InChI\ninchi = cirpy.resolve('aspirin', 'inchi')\n```\n\n## Supported Representations\n- `'smiles'` - SMILES string\n- `'inchi'` - InChI string\n- `'iupac_name'` - IUPAC name\n- `'formula'` - Molecular formula\n- `'mw'` - Molecular weight\n\n## Fallback Strategy\n```python\nimport pubchempy as pcp\nimport cirpy\n\ndef robust_name_to_smiles(name):\n    \"\"\"Try PubChem first, fall back to CIR.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        compounds = pcp.get_compounds(name, 'name')\n        if compounds:\n            return compounds[0].canonical_smiles\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n\n    try:\n        smiles = cirpy.resolve(name, 'smiles')\n        if smiles:\n            return smiles\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n\n    return None\n```\n\n## Notes\n- CIR is maintained by NIH/NCI.\n- No authentication required.\n- Can be slower than PubChem for batch queries.\n- Good fallback for names PubChem doesn't recognize.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0510d43c-6a75-4605-8e29-4f8214c64e0c"}
